//? Title: Burrows–Wheeler: A Reversible Byte Permutation
//? Subtitle: Actual rotation order, stable LF occurrences, binary round trips and measured run structure
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Sorting cyclic rotations turns banana into the last column nnbaaa, with primary
row three. Those bytes and that index recover the original without a sentinel.
We retain this example and the original 59-byte repeated sentence, using native
C++ records for sorted starts, LF occurrences, inverse writes and actual forward
comparison work. The conversion fixes inconsistent signed-byte ordering and
checks invalid primary indices. Run measurements show both clustering and a
counterexample: aabb gains runs after transformation. BWT preserves byte counts
and frequencies; downstream coding, rather than the permutation alone, provides
compression.
)

\toc

> Sort the cyclic rotations of banana

A row starts at an input offset and wraps around after the last byte. Sort
all six rows in unsigned-byte lexicographic order, then keep their final
bytes. The original rotation starts at offset zero and occupies row three.
No special terminator is appended, so the input length remains six.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bwt_rotations.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::bwt_transform_trace("banana");
  if (trace.result.transformed!="nnbaaa" || trace.result.index!=3 || dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse(trace.result)!="banana") return 1;
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  diagram.text({0,1.1},"sorted cyclic rotations",18).text({4.3,1.1},"last byte L",18);
  std::print("| sorted row | original start | displayed cyclic rotation | last byte | original row |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for (std::size_t row=0;row<trace.rotation_starts.size();++row) {
    const auto start=trace.rotation_starts[row];
    const auto rotation=trace.input.substr(start)+trace.input.substr(0,start);
    const double y=-1.15*static_cast<double>(row);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;style.width=3.4;style.height=.85;style.padding=7;
    style.fill=row==trace.result.index?dm::RGB{220,252,231}:dm::RGB{239,246,255};
    diagram.node(std::format("row{}",row),{0,y},std::format("{}: {}",row,rotation),style);
    style.width=1.0;
    diagram.node(std::format("last{}",row),{4.3,y},std::string(1,trace.result.transformed[row]),style)
        .edge(std::format("row{}",row),std::format("last{}",row));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",row,start,rotation,trace.result.transformed[row],row==trace.result.index);
  }
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle summary;summary.width=4.2;summary.height=2.9;summary.fill={254,249,195};
  diagram.node("pair",{8.4,-2.8},std::format("last column: {}\nprimary row: {}\n{} input bytes, {} L bytes",
      trace.result.transformed,trace.result.index,trace.input.size(),trace.result.transformed.size()),summary)
      .save_svg("build/bwt_rotations.svg");
  std::print("\nActual sorted starts: {}. Inverse: {}.\n",trace.rotation_starts,dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse(trace.result));
}
```
