\image(figures/bwt_lf_walk.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The trace records actual writes, not a second inversion. The final next-row value is three; decoding stops because six bytes have been written, rather than because a graph search found a cycle.)
\alttext(Six connected boxes follow LF rows three, zero, four, one, five and two, writing output positions five down to zero. The final output is banana.)

Stable byte groups occupy disjoint contiguous intervals of F, so LF is a
permutation of row ids. A single LF cycle is not required. For example,
abab has equal rotations and an LF walk of period two, but reconstructing
all four bytes requires four writes. A constant string has an identity
LF permutation: the selected row repeats at every step. Stopping at the
first repeated row would lose most of either string.

> Original sentence: fewer runs, unchanged bytes

Keep the original 59-byte input: the quick brown fox repeated three times,
with spaces between copies. A run means a maximal nonempty stretch of
one byte. Fewer runs at the same length means longer average runs, n/R.
The previous comments claiming that *more* such runs help this example
had the direction reversed.

The report reuses native run-length and move-to-front APIs. It does not
reimplement their algorithms. Each MTF output is an alphabet position;
there are still n positions, without a serialized compressed-size claim.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bwt_runs.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string text="the quick brown fox the quick brown fox the quick brown fox";
  const auto transformed=dm::algorithms::bwt_transform(text);
  const auto before=dm::algorithms::rle_encode(text);
  const auto after=dm::algorithms::rle_encode(transformed.transformed);
  std::print("| input | input bytes | last-column bytes | runs before | runs after BWT | MTF zeros before | MTF zeros after | round trip |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& [name,input]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::string>>{
      {"original sentence",text},{"banana","banana"},{"aabb counterexample","aabb"},{"constant","aaaa"}}) {
    const auto encoded=dm::algorithms::bwt_transform(input);
    const auto original_runs=dm::algorithms::rle_encode(input).size();
    const auto transformed_runs=dm::algorithms::rle_encode(encoded.transformed).size();
    const auto original_mtf=dm::algorithms::mtf_encode(input);
    const auto transformed_mtf=dm::algorithms::mtf_encode(encoded.transformed);
    const auto zero_before=std::count(original_mtf.begin(),original_mtf.end(),0);
    const auto zero_after=std::count(transformed_mtf.begin(),transformed_mtf.end(),0);
    const bool round_trip=dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse(encoded)==input;
    if (!round_trip || encoded.transformed.size()!=input.size() ||
        zero_before!=static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(input.size()-original_runs) ||
        zero_after!=static_cast<std::ptrdiff_t>(input.size()-transformed_runs)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,input.size(),encoded.transformed.size(),
        original_runs,transformed_runs,zero_before,zero_after,round_trip);
  }
  if (text.size()!=59 || before.size()!=59 || after.size()!=20) return 1;
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot({static_cast<double>(before.size()),static_cast<double>(after.size())},
      {"before BWT","after BWT"},"run count",dm::RGB{147,51,234})
      .title("Original sentence: 59 runs become 20")
      .x_label("stage").y_label("maximal same-byte runs").size(1000,480)
      .save_svg("build/bwt_runs.svg");
  std::print("\nOriginal sentence mean run lengths: {:.2f} before, {:.2f} after. Run records are not serialized bytes.\n",
      static_cast<double>(text.size())/before.size(),static_cast<double>(text.size())/after.size());
}
