\image(figures/bwt_runs.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original 59-versus-20 run-count plot is retained and its interpretation corrected. The control aabb becomes baba, increasing runs from two to four; BWT does not guarantee improved run counts on every block.)
\alttext(Two purple bars show fifty-nine maximal runs before BWT and twenty afterward for the same fifty-nine input bytes.)

Sorting rows places related following contexts together, collecting their
preceding bytes in L. That can help a local coder exploit context-dependent
regularity. It does not reduce zero-order empirical entropy: every byte
frequency p_c is preserved, so minus-sum p_c log2(p_c) is unchanged.
A run-count change is therefore not a byte-frequency compression ratio.

After a byte is MTF-encoded, it becomes the alphabet's front element. Every
later identical adjacent byte consequently has rank zero. For the native
initial alphabet zero through 255, a byte string with n bytes and R runs has

\[
Z=n-R+\mathbf 1_{\{n>0\;\mathrm{and}\;s[0]=0\}}
\]

zero-valued MTF positions. All displayed text cases start with a nonzero
byte, so Z=n-R. The sentence gains 39 zero positions after BWT. Counting
those positions illustrates what a later zero-run coder might exploit;
it does not implement that coder or establish a final compressed size.

BWT is used in [bzip2](https://sourceware.org/bzip2/manual/manual.html), whose
format includes additional coding, metadata and integrity machinery. These
native cyclic-transform pairs and MTF vectors are not bzip2 files, and the
report does not claim to reproduce its complete pipeline.

> Actual forward comparison work and linear inverse writes

The core stores rotation starts rather than full strings, but each naive
comparison may inspect n byte pairs. Let C(n) be the actual number of
std::sort comparator calls and B(n) the number of inspected byte pairs.
For a constant input every comparison sees n equal bytes, then uses the
start-offset tie break. Therefore B(n)=n*C(n) exactly for this family.
The resulting worst-case upper bound is O(n^2 log(n+1)) time and O(n)
auxiliary storage. Sorting-call counts are toolchain-dependent; the report
measures them instead of assigning a universal exact formula.

LF construction counts a fixed 256-byte alphabet and visits n positions.
The inverse then performs exactly n writes, giving O(n+256) time and
storage. Tracing adds owned provenance and write records, still linear
storage; normal APIs do not allocate those records. It is not necessary
to build or retain an n-by-n rotation matrix for either core operation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bwt_comparison_work.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> x,calls,byte_pairs,writes;
  std::print("| unary input bytes n | actual rotation comparisons C | actual byte-pair tests B | checked n*C | full-equality comparisons | inverse writes |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const std::size_t n:{8,16,32,64,128,256,512}) {
    const auto forward=dm::algorithms::bwt_transform_trace(std::string(n,'a'));
    const auto inverse=dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse_trace(forward.result);
    if (forward.byte_comparisons!=n*forward.rotation_comparisons ||
        forward.equal_rotation_comparisons!=forward.rotation_comparisons || inverse.steps.size()!=n ||
        inverse.output!=forward.input) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,forward.rotation_comparisons,forward.byte_comparisons,
        n*forward.rotation_comparisons,forward.equal_rotation_comparisons,inverse.steps.size());
    x.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(n)));
    calls.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(forward.rotation_comparisons)));
    byte_pairs.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(forward.byte_comparisons)));
    writes.push_back(std::log2(static_cast<double>(inverse.steps.size())));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(x,byte_pairs,"byte-pair tests",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2).points(x,byte_pairs,"",dm::RGB{220,38,38},4)
      .lines(x,calls,"rotation calls",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2).points(x,calls,"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},4)
      .lines(x,writes,"inverse writes",dm::RGB{22,163,74},2).points(x,writes,"",dm::RGB{22,163,74},4)
      .title("Naive rotations: n byte-pair tests per unary comparison")
      .x_label("log2(input bytes n)").y_label("log2(actual operation count)").y_limits(0,27)
      .size(1100,520).save_svg("build/bwt_comparison_work.svg");
}
```
