\caption(All logarithms are applied explicitly to positive observed counts. The gap between byte tests and comparator calls is exactly log2(n). The inverse curve counts writes, without claiming that LF construction or metadata operations take zero time.)
\alttext(On logarithmic axes, unary forward byte-pair tests grow much faster than rotation calls, while inverse writes grow exactly linearly with input length.)

> Periodicity, binary bytes, malformed indices and budgets

An LF permutation alone does not certify that an arbitrary pair belongs to
the cyclic-transform image. The inverse validates the primary index before
allocation or indexing, but it is not a corruption detector. For example,
the in-range pair (ab,0) decodes deterministically to aa; transforming aa
produces a different last column. A stricter validator would need additional
semantic checks and is not silently implied by range validation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::string binary;for (unsigned int value:{0,128,255,0,255}) binary.push_back(static_cast<char>(value));
  std::print("| case | bytes | primary row | LF permutation | inverse writes | round trip |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (const auto& [name,input]:std::vector<std::pair<std::string,std::string>>{
      {"empty",""},{"singleton","x"},{"periodic abab","abab"},{"unary aaaa","aaaa"},{"binary 00 80 FF 00 FF",binary}}) {
    const auto encoded=dm::algorithms::bwt_transform(input);
    const auto decoded=dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse_trace(encoded);
    const bool round_trip=decoded.output==input;
    if (!round_trip || decoded.steps.size()!=input.size()) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",name,input.size(),encoded.index,decoded.mapping.lf,decoded.steps.size(),round_trip);
  }
  const dm::algorithms::BWTResult unary{std::string(200000,'x'),0};
  const auto large=dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse_trace(unary);
  const bool repeats_zero=std::all_of(large.steps.begin(),large.steps.end(),[](const auto& step){return step.row==0;});
  if (large.output!=unary.transformed || large.steps.size()!=200000 || !repeats_zero) return 1;
  std::print("\nKnown unary encoding: {} bytes, {} actual writes; every visited row is zero: {}.\n",
      unary.transformed.size(),large.steps.size(),repeats_zero);
  bool invalid_empty=false,invalid_row=false,budget=false;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse(dm::algorithms::BWTResult{"",1});}
  catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {invalid_empty=true;}
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse_trace(dm::algorithms::BWTResult{"a",1});}
  catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {invalid_row=true;}
  dm::algorithms::BWTForwardOptions options;options.max_byte_comparisons=1;
  try {(void)dm::algorithms::bwt_transform_trace("banana",options);}
  catch (const std::runtime_error&) {budget=true;}
  if (!invalid_empty || !invalid_row || !budget) return 1;
  std::print("Invalid empty index, out-of-range row and one-byte-pair budget rejected: {}, {}, {}.\n",
      invalid_empty,invalid_row,budget);
  const dm::algorithms::BWTResult nonimage{"ab",0};
  const auto arbitrary=dm::algorithms::bwt_inverse(nonimage);
  std::print("In-range non-image pair (ab,0) decodes to {}; retransform reproduces supplied L: {}.\n",
      arbitrary,dm::algorithms::bwt_transform(arbitrary).transformed==nonimage.transformed);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | bytes | primary row | LF permutation | inverse writes | round trip |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- | ---: | :--- |
| empty | 0 | 0 | [] | 0 | true |
| singleton | 1 | 0 | [0] | 1 | true |
| periodic abab | 4 | 0 | [2, 3, 0, 1] | 4 | true |
| unary aaaa | 4 | 0 | [0, 1, 2, 3] | 4 | true |
| binary 00 80 FF 00 FF | 5 | 0 | [3, 4, 0, 1, 2] | 5 | true |

Known unary encoding: 200000 bytes, 200000 actual writes; every visited row is zero: true.
Invalid empty index, out-of-range row and one-byte-pair budget rejected: true, true, true.
In-range non-image pair (ab,0) decodes to aa; retransform reproduces supplied L: false.
// result_end

The 200,000-byte control constructs a known valid unary encoding directly;
it does not run the deliberately naive forward transform on that large
block. It verifies that an inverse walk can revisit one row 200,000 times
and still produce the full output. For an empty pair, the canonical primary
index is zero; default-constructed BWTResult now initializes it safely.

Forward trace budgets count actual inspected byte pairs and throw before an
excess comparison, returning no partial result. Counts use size_t throughout;
the inverse no longer narrows frequencies and cumulative offsets into int.
The caller's input remains unchanged on success or exception.

Native checks use an independent materialized rotation matrix and a separate
prepend-and-sort inverse on small inputs. All 9,841 strings over the byte
alphabet {0,128,255} through length eight, all 256 byte values and 1,000 random
binary/periodic inputs match the canonical transform and actual counters.
Stable occurrence-pair sorting independently checks LF, while boundary indices,
exact comparison budgets, periodic writes, run/MTF controls and existing
compression/format tests cover the rest. This mechanism report uses explicit
and generated byte blocks, without an artificial dataset requirement.

> References

- M. Burrows and D. J. Wheeler (1994), [A Block-sorting Lossless Data Compression Algorithm](https://www.cs.jhu.edu/~langmea/resources/burrows_wheeler.pdf),
  Digital Systems Research Center Research Report 124, 10 May. Section 2 gives
  the cyclic last-column/primary-index transform and stable predecessor mapping.
- J. Seward, [bzip2 and libbzip2 manual](https://sourceware.org/bzip2/manual/manual.html),
  for the distinction between a block-sorting transform and the complete bzip2
  compression format and library.
